And so again, the whole point of this is it simplifies the deduplication and integration of records downstream business units, for example, in the hospital again

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:22.000
That very easy way to bring together all the treatments that you've had in different departments of the hospital at different times into a single medical chart

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:29.000
So that's the management master data. It's a very big operation, and there are many vendors in this space

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:34.000
Now, transactional data is sort of like the verbs, right? There's something happening

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:46.000
with those entities, right? So, for example, a good example would be, like, a sales order or credit card payment, or insurance claim. What's happening is

00:25:46.000 --> 00:26:01.000
You've got a sales associate who's an employee that has an employee number, and the transaction is sold a product which has also is in master data management. So again, just like in a language, the

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:06.000
The transactional data sort of represents sentences where you have subject

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:19.000
verbs and objects, right? A sales associate sells a product to a… and again there would also be a customer

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:24.000
So, the sales associate and the product, the customer number are all under Master Data Management

00:26:24.000 --> 00:26:30.000
We want a unique identifiers for each one of our customers, unique identifiers for each one of our products

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:38.000
Each one of our employees and then in this case, we want a lot of duplication

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:49.000
In Master Data Management, we don't want duplicates. We don't want to put the same customer in the master data file twice because now it looks like two customers instead of one

00:26:49.000 --> 00:27:01.000
So we really want to deduplicate our master files. We want one-to-one correspondence, one master record for each entity that we're tracking.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:03.000
If it's a patient, a customer, a product

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:17.000
On the other hand, transactional data, we want lots of it, right? We would like to sell lots of product P456, right? The more we sell, the more profits that we can make. So there, it's different. We want lots of transactions

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:28.000
But by embedding semantically encoding those transactions with the identifiers of the master data, then it makes it very easy for us to

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:43.000
to do our downstream systems where we're making invoices or making sales reports, inventory, we can track those things because we have those unique identifiers.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:48.000
And then the third type of data is reference data

00:27:48.000 --> 00:28:00.000
And reference data is really very much like master data, except it's simpler in the sense that it really deals with code or classification schemes

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.000
That are referenced by transaction records

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:08.000
or master record, so it it

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:16.000
What reference data is, is an agreement on what codes we're going to use so that data becomes interoperable

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:19.000
Take, for example, here at the university

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:31.000
Well, you're not here at the university, many of you, but we have codes that represent the buildings on campus, right? So right now I'm in

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.000
EIT building

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:35.000
And

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:50.000
The electronics or engineering, I'm sorry, engineering and information technology building. It's abbreviated as EIT. But we want to use that. We want to consistently use that code so that there's no confusion

00:28:50.000 --> 00:29:02.000
If somebody else is using a different code, then, again, it's the same problem as Master Data Management. But in the idea of reference data is that we don't have to semantically encode it

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:10.000
In other words, I don't want to go to another step and another abstraction and say, well, here's the engineering and information technology building

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:14.000
I don't want to sign… try to assign it a

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:25.000
a numeric identifier to track it because it's very simple. If I just consistently use EIT, it's a single value

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:33.000
The same thing with postal codes, right? So Arkansas is standard postal code is capital A, capital R.

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:48.000
And so, as long as we consistently use that in an address context, it's clear that if I see AR, it means Arkansas, and if I want to use Arkansas, I should encode it as AR.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:57.000
So it's somewhat similar to master data, but it's a little simpler in that we're really dealing mainly with codes

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:09.000
And by using the same codes, for example, in courses here on campus, everybody understands that when they see the code EIT by a class listing or course listing

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:15.000
They know that it's referring to a particular building on campus

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:25.000
And so reference data is very helpful, very key to data integration and data interoperability, just like master data

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:35.000
So we see a lot of those postal codes, the universal product codes, things like that. So some of the codes may be from outside

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:46.000
that are kind of standard. For example, ISO has many, many standard codes for geographic, like the codes that we were talking about for states, but also

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:49.000
countries, for

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:52.000
For

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:56.000
The money, currency codes

00:30:56.000 --> 00:31:06.000
And so forth. So there are many sort of agreed-on nationally or international for postal codes would be within the United States are agreed upon

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:13.000
ISO codes would be, you know, universally agreed upon by all countries that participate in ISO

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:20.000
So those are external, but again, we often have internal code, just like I was talking about

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:27.000
Unfortunately, ISO doesn't have a set of codes for the buildings on the UA Little Rock campus

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:42.000
We build those and maintain those on our own, as well as our job codes here, so forth and so on. So, the reference data could be that we require it to be some kind of national or international standard

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:48.000
Or we invent our own for our particular context.

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:54.000
So metadata, the fourth type of data

00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:02.000
Metadata literally means data about data. So, again, if you have raw data values

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:04.000
Then

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:11.000
They can be very ambiguous, right? I have a date

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:14.000
That I have a date value

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:24.000
If I just give you a date value, then you know intrinsically what it means, and if you recognize the format, and you say, well, oh

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:31.000
This is July the 4th, 1959. I recognize that by the layout

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:46.000
But what is the meaning? What is the semantics of that? I mean, it means a particular date in the calendar, but in a particular application, what does it mean? Is it a date of birth? Is it a date of a sales transaction? Could be many, many things

00:32:46.000 --> 00:32:50.000
So metadata helps to clear that up

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:54.000
If I have a field of dates and I label it as

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:59.000
dates of birth, and I know that what I'm describing with those dates

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:03.000
The dates of birth were the associated entity.

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:12.000
And so they label, describe, and characterize other data to make it easier to retrieve, interpret, or use

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:21.000
Technical metadata is data that and business metadata are two major types of metadata

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:29.000
Technical metadata describes technology systems, data structures, data quality rules, data standards

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:34.000
Business data is more about how the operations are to be performed. What are the rules

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:49.000
Can a product be returned? Does it require receipt? Can it be returned after 30 days? Different types of rules about the conduct of the business

00:33:49.000 --> 00:33:57.000
Again, I keep saying business, but this could be any organization. It could be like a hospital or it could be like a government agency.

00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:01.000
So those are the two different types of

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:04.000
metadata that we typically encounter

00:34:04.000 --> 00:34:11.000
And there are many metadata management tools

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:16.000
One has become very popular now with the

00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:18.000
And metadata

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:23.000
It has become very important in the area of data governance

00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:26.000
So as we mentioned earlier in this lecture

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:31.000
Data governance are the rules for making decisions about data.

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:33.000
What rules do you have to follow

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:35.000
And so

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:42.000
In some ways, data governance is about managing metadata

00:34:42.000 --> 00:34:49.000
So, data catalogs are intended to describe the data that you have in an organization

00:34:49.000 --> 00:35:02.000
track what's in it, the description of what's in the data, where it's located, what are its characteristics. So data catalogs have become very important and they're huge tools that vendors have

00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:10.000
To help in tracking data, because if you don't know the metadata, the data are pretty much useless to you, right? You

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:13.000
And we have I wrote an article

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:18.000
A few years ago called data littering

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:25.000
And it was at a time when there was a lot of emphasis on data literacy. So people thought I just misspoke

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:31.000
And so I meant to say data literacy, but I said data literary. But no, I meant it

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:40.000
Explicitly. And what I was talking about is data littering is distributing data without metadata

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:54.000
You have a file, you're working with it, you and a buddy know what's going on, you're exchanging the file back and forth with a spreadsheet or something, but it really doesn't have adequate description of what's in the data, when it was captured, what it was for

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:59.000
And that gets out there somewhere. Somebody finds it, or it gets on

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:07.000
a thumb drive or something, and you're basically littering data around because you kind of… it's like throwing

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:12.000
wrapper out of a car onto the street you know it's

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:23.000
You've thrown it out there, no, and what's the… it's not useful, right? It's not useful anymore because it doesn't have that metadata associated with it. So I call

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:26.000
They know littering

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:37.000
Business glossary. So data catalogs tend to be like data dictionaries that really describe the content of a particular file or a particular database

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:39.000
Business glossaries

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:44.000
are more on the side of understanding how that data

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:51.000
So some data is sort of native data. In other words, if you take payroll data, right

00:36:51.000 --> 00:37:04.000
So the native data in a payroll system would be the data about who's the employee, how many hours did they work, or are they on a salary, are they hourly? The information describing that

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:12.000
But then those items of data are sort of given items, native items, that come out of

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:14.000
the system

00:37:14.000 --> 00:37:19.000
And so… but you're going to generate data off of that data

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:33.000
You're going to take those hours, you're going to take the rate of pay and so forth, and you're going to compute a new data value, which is the gross pay, the net pay, the deduction. There'll be a whole nother set of items

00:37:33.000 --> 00:37:41.000
that did not come into the company through some file. Their data that's generated to run the business

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:56.000
And so, the business glossary should describe those items that are not necessarily out there in a file, but are generated in the operation of the company and exactly how those are generated, what was the logic in generating

00:37:56.000 --> 00:38:08.000
What was the logic? How was your net pay calculated? When I say this item of value or this data value is your net pay, you need something that describes

00:38:08.000 --> 00:38:17.000
Beyond just saying this is what's going to be on your paycheck, you need something that says, how did I calculate that

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:24.000
So there's processes as well that metadata documents that run

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:30.000
systems, how those systems are to be run, what are the rules for running those

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:38.000
And business process management tools that automate. And this is another place where AI is starting to come into

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:46.000
The picture because now what we're looking at is what we call Agentic AI

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:51.000
And in the early days, it's only been a couple of years ago

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:54.000
of large language models

00:38:54.000 --> 00:39:09.000
you know, I mean, it's very fascinating. But they were more prompt in response, right? It's like having a dialogue. We call them chatbots, right? So we type something in, some sentence or some question, could be long or give it a document

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:13.000
And we ask it a question about, you know, what

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:18.000
What should I do here if I've got these symptoms

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:24.000
And it would think about it, and it would give a response. So it's this idea of prompt and response, prompt and response

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:28.000
But now there's another

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:32.000
facet that's been developed, and that is this idea of

00:39:32.000 --> 00:39:40.000
The large language model being able to do something rather than just respond in text

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:50.000
You can actually ask it to do something, and it can operate other systems. It can go in and read something from a database, or it can write an email

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:54.000
or can ride a ticket that goes in your Jira system

00:39:54.000 --> 00:39:58.000
So it can be connected with other systems to do things

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:01.000
And not only that, it can be

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:10.000
You can make decisions. It can evaluate and basically become like an employee in the company

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:15.000
to doing things, and so now this has opened up a whole new world of Agentic AI

00:40:15.000 --> 00:40:19.000
Where people are creating these little

00:40:19.000 --> 00:40:34.000
computer employees that work inside the computer, and they can do things. They can book an airline ticket for you, and they can evaluate, make decisions. They can say, no, there's no seats available in this, I need to go back

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:46.000
That's another way to get to Chicago. Is there another flight? All the things that you would do if you were sitting at a console trying to book tickets for your airline

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:59.000
You can get agents just like a real person travel agent. You can have a computer agent and large language model that can go through and make those kind of decisions and loop back on itself.

00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:02.000
So this has opened up quite a bit

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:10.000
versatility. Again, the only big issue that we're running into there is, well, there's a couple of them. One is

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:18.000
Again, the large language models, even though they can reason, are probabilistic models and sometimes

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:27.000
They… what we call hallucinating, which is really nothing more than making a bad prediction. Language models are predictive machines that

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:35.000
You give it words, and they try to predict what the next word is going to be. So they're very probabilistic in their operation

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:51.000
And so they make mistakes. So, again, they are very human in that they can do human things of making human-like decisions, but they can also make human-like mistakes as well. So one issue is how do you put up guardrails to be sure that

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:57.000
These little workers don't go crazy on you and do something that would really harm your business

00:41:57.000 --> 00:42:02.000
And the other is, again, it's the same issue. They're slow

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:08.000
And I say slow, I mean it may seem very fast that it's operating in seconds

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:24.000
But in terms of what we're used to with our current legacy systems that are rule-based, that are deterministic and not probabilistic, that their operations take place in milliseconds or microseconds

00:42:24.000 --> 00:42:32.000
And so they can process vast amounts of data in a reasonable amount of time. But that becomes an issue for AI models

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:36.000
So we're dealing with

