So data standards, those are a collection of related data requirements to ensure that everyone is organizing and acting in a consistent way.

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:36.000
The way you name data, the way you define it, the way you format it

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:44.000
The way you validate it and you follow specific business rules. Those are standards in an organization

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:50.000
So we have data models, typically we have relational models. Those are called schemas. But now

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:54.000
These models have become a little more open

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:56.000
And

00:44:56.000 --> 00:45:00.000
One problem with data models is that

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:04.000
There's famous statistician named George Box

00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:08.000
And he'd made this comment become quite famous

00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:11.000
He said that

00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:14.000
models are wrong, but some are useful

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:23.000
And that tends to be true in the traditional way that we process data. We set up a model, typically it's a relational database model

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:31.000
And we try to think ahead and say, you know, will this accommodate everything that's going to happen? And of course, it never does

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:41.000
Things change, the data changes. And so we constantly having to sort of update our models to keep them where they're still useful to us.

00:45:41.000 --> 00:45:48.000
Because we're always going to be wrong once we set a model, because traditionally what we do is we take data

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:54.000
And we try to transform it to fit into the model. So we lose the original data

00:45:54.000 --> 00:46:00.000
And this is what's led to the modern idea of data warehouses or data lakes

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:09.000
Especially the idea of a data lake, is that you take the original raw data and you preserve it in a data lake

00:46:09.000 --> 00:46:23.000
Then pull it out and transform it the way you want to fit it into your model, so that you can always go back to the original data in case there is a problem or a mistake.

